    "score": 0.0,
    "reasoning": "Nitpicks: (1) Prices shown in euros rather than INR is not an issue; Google Flights can display different currencies. (2) The three lowest visible options being the same airline (IndiGo) is acceptable because the task says “three lowest visible options,” and ties can lead to same-airline comparisons.\n\nCritical issues: The task asks to compare round-trip options by “departure time” (for a round trip, a reasonable interpretation is outbound and return timing). The agent only provided outbound departure/arrival times and explicitly noted that Google Flights “only showing the outbound leg details” in that view. Because return-leg departure times are not provided, the comparison is missing a key schedule component for a true round-trip evaluation.\n\nOutcome assessment: Screenshot evidence (latest states 13–14) supports that the agent did run the correct Google Flights search (DEL→BLR round trip, Sep 15–18 2026, economy, 1 passenger) and applied the nonstop filter (Stops chip shows Nonstop selected). The three lowest visible nonstop results are indeed €154 round trip, IndiGo, with the three different outbound departure times (3:00 AM, 8:45 PM, 10:00 PM), all 2h55m, and the agent’s reported fares/times/duration align with what’s visible. The recommendation (8:45 PM option as best balance) is reasonable given identical prices/durations and the other two being an extreme early departure or post-midnight arrival.\n\nHowever, because the user asked for a round trip and explicitly wanted schedule comparison, not having any return-leg timing (and therefore basing “best balance of price and schedule” only on outbound) is a meaningful omission. The agent did acknowledge the limitation, but it still means the user does not receive the full comparison they requested for a round-trip itinerary. So this is more than a nitpick: it reduces task completion from the user’s perspective.",
